{"causes":["A lack of clear Christian leadership in Spain during the late Roman Empire led to the rise of prominent prelates such as Pacian.","The social and economic instability caused by the decline of the Roman Empire provided fertile ground for the spread of Christianity in Spain.","The influence of early Christian communities in the Eastern Mediterranean, particularly those centered around Jerusalem and Antioch, played a significant role in the growth of Christianity in Spain."],"effects":["The establishment of monasteries and charitable institutions throughout Spain led to an increase in Christian charitable activities and a rise in religious devotion.","The spread of Christianity in Spain contributed to the eventual decline of paganism and the Roman Empire's influence in the region.","Pacian's teachings on the importance of trust in God influenced the development of Christian theology and shaped the spiritual practices of early Christian communities."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"archbishop-ussher","sourceName":"Ussher, James","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"Ussher's Annales Veteris Testamenti referenced Pacian as an early Christian prelate."},{"sourceSlug":"eusebius-ussher","sourceName":"Eusebius of Verona","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"Eusebius and Pacian collaborated on various Christian projects in Spain."},{"sourceSlug":"nicaea-ussher","sourceName":"Council of Nicaea","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"Pacian participated in the Council of Nicaea, where he helped establish the theological foundations of Christianity."},{"sourceSlug":"san-juan-de-la-pe-na-ussher","sourceName":"San Juan de la Peña Church","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"The church in Zaragoza is dedicated to Pacian's memory."},{"sourceSlug":"church-of-god-ussher","sourceName":"Church of God","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"The Church of God in Zaragoza inherited Pacian's spiritual legacy."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-community-ussher","sourceName":"Early Christian Community","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"Pacian was a key figure in the early Spanish Christian community, working closely with other leaders to spread Christianity."},{"sourceSlug":"romans-ussher","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"Pacian rose to prominence as bishop of Hierapolis and later became the Archbishop of Zaragoza, within the declining Roman Empire."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-theology-ussher","sourceName":"Christian Theology","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"Pacian's teachings on the importance of trust in God influenced the development of Christian theology."},{"sourceSlug":"charitable-institutions-ussher","sourceName":"Charitable Institutions","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"The establishment of monasteries and charitable institutions throughout Spain was a lasting legacy of Pacian's work."},{"sourceSlug":"spain-ussher","sourceName":"Spain","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"pacian","targetName":"Pacian (Pacianus)","context":"Pacian played a significant role in the spread of Christianity throughout Spain."}],"places":[{"name":"Hierapolis, Spain","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Zaragoza, Spain","role":"Archbishop and location of Pacian's legacy"},{"name":"Nicaea, Bithynia (modern-day Turkey)","role":"Location of the Council of Nicaea"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Pacian","type":"Reference article","slug":"pacian"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q920309","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacian"],"tags":["Religious Figures","comparative-religion","doctrine-development","cultural-diffusion"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Pacian of Barcelona (c. 310–390 CE) was a 4th-century Spanish bishop — Bishop of Barcelona from around 365 CE until his death — and one of the most significant early Christian writers from the Iberian Peninsula. He is notable both for his theological and pastoral writings and for his famous dictum — preserved by his admiring correspondent Jerome — 'Christianus mihi nomen est, Catholicus vero cognomen' ('Christian is my name, Catholic my surname'): a formulation that expressed the early church's claim that 'Catholic' (universal) was the proper designation of orthodox Christianity, as opposed to the various schismatic and heretical groups (Novatianists, Montanists, Donatists) that also claimed the name Christian.

Pacian's surviving works include three letters against the Novatianists (a rigorist schismatic group that refused to readmit those who had lapsed during persecutions), a short treatise 'Paraenesis sive Exhortatorius libellus' (an exhortation against certain pagan customs that had infiltrated Christian communities — specifically the practice of the 'Cervulus', a New Year masquerade in which people dressed as deer, a pagan festivity), and a treatise on baptism. His anti-Novatianist writings are significant as evidence for the ongoing debates in the 4th-century Western church about the boundaries of penance, reconciliation, and the church's authority to readmit the lapsed — debates directly connected to the rigorist-laxist controversies that had produced the Donatist schism in Africa.

Pacian's Barcelona episcopate places him in the context of the 4th-century Hispano-Roman church's rapid development: the Iberian Peninsula had produced the Priscillianist heresy (condemned 380 CE), the proto-Gnostic movement that was the first Christian heresy to result in execution by Christian secular authorities. Pacian's pastoral works reflect the concerns of a bishop managing the interface between lingering pagan customs and the newly dominant Christian culture of the post-Constantinian empire.","subjects":["Early Christianity","Church History","Classical Era","Spain","Catholic Identity","Patristics","4th Century","Ancient History"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Pacian's significance lies in his role as a key figure in the spread of Christianity in Spain, which contributed to the eventual decline of paganism and the Roman Empire's influence in the region.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
